explanation:
The main surgical treatments for bladder cancer include: 
① Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT): for Ta, T₁, and localized T₂ stage tumors (correct answer: A); 
② Partial cystectomy or radical cystectomy: for larger T₂ stage tumors (correct answer: B); 
③ Radical cystectomy: for recurrent, multiple tumors or those invading the bladder neck and trigone area (correct answer: D), with the scope of resection including the bladder, prostate, and seminal vesicles (incorrect answer: C, which is the correct answer for this question), along with pelvic lymph node dissection; 
④ Ileal conduit: performed after radical cystectomy (correct answer: E) for urinary diversion and bladder substitution.

According to the depth of tumor infiltration, the TNM staging of bladder cancer is as follows:  
Tis refers to carcinoma in situ;  
Ta (incorrectly marked as D) refers to non-invasive papillary carcinoma;  
T1 (incorrectly marked as A) refers to tumor infiltration into the lamina propria;  
T2 (incorrectly marked as B) refers to tumor infiltration into the muscle layer, which can be further divided into T2a (correctly marked as E), infiltration of the superficial muscle layer (inner half of the muscle), and T2b, infiltration of the deep muscle layer (outer half of the muscle);  
T3 refers to tumor infiltration into the perivesical fat tissue, which can be further divided into T3a (incorrectly marked as C), where microscopic tumor invasion of perivesical tissue is detected, and T3b, where macroscopic tumor invasion of perivesical tissue is visible;  
T4 refers to infiltration of adjacent organs such as the prostate, uterus, vagina, and pelvic wall.

According to the depth of tumor invasion, the TNM staging criteria for bladder cancer are as follows: 
① Tis: Carcinoma in situ; 
② Ta: Non-invasive papillary carcinoma; 
③ T1: Invasion into the lamina propria; 
④ T2: Invasion into the muscle layer (T2a: Invasion into the superficial muscle layer, T2b: Invasion into the deep muscle layer); 
⑤ T3: Invasion into the perivesical fat; 
⑥ T4: Invasion into adjacent organs such as the prostate, uterus, vagina, and pelvic wall. Based on the question stem, the correct answer is B.
